home-library for Debian and Ubuntu
==================================

How it runs
-----------

systemd owns the socket, /run/home-library/home-library.sock, and starts the
app (home-library.service) on the first request. The socket belongs to the
home-library user and group www-data, mode 0660, so nginx and Apache, which
run as www-data, can connect and nothing else can. The app never listens on a
TCP port.

  Code      /usr/lib/home-library
  Data      /var/lib/home-library (library.db and covers/)
  Settings  /etc/home-library/home-library.env   (yours; root:home-library 0640)
            /etc/home-library/secret.env         (generated SESSION_SECRET)

The service sets BASE_PATH=/library and TRUST_PROXY=true, matching the web
server configuration below. Set PUBLIC_ORIGIN in home-library.env if sign-in
redirects or emailed links come out with the wrong scheme or host.

Purging the package keeps /var/lib/home-library: the catalogue is yours to
delete.

Apache
------

The package enables mod_proxy, mod_proxy_http and mod_headers and the
home-library configuration (/etc/apache2/conf-available/home-library.conf),
which serves the app at /library/ on every virtual host. To limit it to one,
disable the conf (a2disconf home-library) and Include the file inside that
<VirtualHost>.

nginx
-----

nginx has no way for a package to add a location to your site, so add one
line inside the server { } block that should serve the library:

    include snippets/home-library.conf;

then reload nginx. Upgrades reload nginx for you once a site includes it.

Camera scanning needs HTTPS: browsers only offer the camera to secure pages.
